---
// New to Linux / Nuevo en Linux
// A friendly explainer for people coming from macOS or Windows:
// what actually changes under the hood (with optional deep-dives),
// and how to drive Pulsar OS from day one. Plain talk, no jargon walls.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

---

<Layout pageKey="new-to-linux">
    <div class="ntl-page">
        <!-- Hero -->
        <section class="ntl-hero" aria-label="New to Linux introduction">
            <div class="container ntl-hero-inner">
                <h1 class="ntl-title">New to Linux? Relax.</h1>
                <p class="ntl-lead">
                    If you have used a Mac or a PC, you already know 90% of what
                    you need. This page explains the other 10% in plain
                    language — and shows you how little there is to it.
                </p>
                <div class="ntl-hero-actions">
                    <a href="#from-macos" class="btn btn-primary">I come from a Mac</a>
                    <a href="#from-windows" class="btn btn-secondary">I come from Windows</a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- From macOS -->
        <section id="from-macos" class="ntl-section" aria-label="Coming from macOS">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 class="section-title">Coming from a Mac</h2>
                <p class="section-subtitle">
                    Good news: Bitten Fruit was built thinking of you. The top
                    bar, the dock, the gestures — all of it works the way your
                    hands already expect. Here is what is actually different.
                </p>

                <div class="ntl-compare">
                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On your Mac</span>
                            <p>Apps come from the App Store, or a .dmg you download and drag.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Bitten Fruit</span>
                            <p>The same idea: the App Store is built in and has more apps. A .dmg becomes a .deb — same drag-and-install feel via the store.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On your Mac</span>
                            <p>Finder with tags, previews and a sidebar.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Bitten Fruit</span>
                            <p>A Finder rebuild with the same navigation, tags and previews — plus your clouds (Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive…) right in the sidebar.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On your Mac</span>
                            <p>Spotlight: one key, search everything.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Bitten Fruit</span>
                            <p>Spotlight is here too, and it can even uninstall apps and jump into folders. Same key, same feel.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On your Mac</span>
                            <p>Time Machine backing up your stuff automatically.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Bitten Fruit</span>
                            <p>Time Machine, same idea: continuous snapshots, saved to a USB drive, a network server or your cloud, and restorable from Recovery.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On your Mac</span>
                            <p>System Settings, and everything in the menu bar.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Bitten Fruit</span>
                            <p>A Settings app rebuilt to look and flow like System Settings, plus a Control Center in the top-right corner. And yes: each app's menus (File, Edit, View…) live in the top bar, like on a Mac.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On your Mac</span>
                            <p>Cmd is the main modifier key.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Bitten Fruit</span>
                            <p>Cmd does what Cmd does — and if you prefer Ctrl+C like on Windows or Linux, one setting swaps the whole keyboard.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <details class="ntl-deepdive">
                    <summary>What is Pulsar OS, really? (the honest one-paragraph version)</summary>
                    <div class="ntl-deepdive-body">
                        <p>
                            Under the hood it is GNU/Linux with the GNOME
                            desktop — but almost nothing you touch is stock.
                            The desktop, the Finder-style file manager, the
                            global menu, the login screen, the boot sequence,
                            the sounds, the keyboard, Spotlight, Time Machine,
                            Recovery, the app store and Sayri are all custom
                            builds made for this system. That is the difference
                            between a theme and an operating system: a theme
                            paints the same old desktop; Pulsar OS reassembles
                            it around the Mac flow, end to end.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </details>

                <details class="ntl-deepdive">
                    <summary>What is actually different under the hood, technically? (optional nerd bit)</summary>
                    <div class="ntl-deepdive-body">
                        <p>
                            A Mac runs macOS. Bitten Fruit runs Linux — a system
                            built by a worldwide community, where thousands of
                            people can read every line of code. That is why it
                            can be free, auditable and never nag you.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The practical differences you may notice one day:
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <strong>Everything is a file.</strong> Settings,
                                drives, even some hardware show up as files you
                                can read. Powerful, but Bitten Fruit gives you
                                normal Settings windows so you never have to
                                care.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <strong>Apps come from repositories.</strong>
                                Instead of one store, apps live in several big
                                catalogues. The App Store merges them all, so
                                from your chair it looks like one store.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <strong>You are the administrator.</strong> No
                                gatekeeper decides what you can install. That
                                freedom is also why Linux asks "are you sure?"
                                a bit less often than macOS — it trusts you.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- From Windows -->
        <section id="from-windows" class="ntl-section" aria-label="Coming from Windows">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 class="section-title">Coming from Windows</h2>
                <p class="section-subtitle">
                    Your habits carry over: taskbar, start-style menus,
                    Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V. And Wintux — the Windows-flow edition — is
                    on its way. Here is what changes meanwhile.
                </p>

                <div class="ntl-compare">
                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Windows</span>
                            <p>Download an .exe from a website, click through the installer.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Pulsar OS</span>
                            <p>Two ways, both easy. The App Store: search, click, installed — updates included. Or download an installer (.deb, like an .exe) and open it by double-clicking, like on Windows.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Windows</span>
                            <p>Taskbar, start menu, tray icons, Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Pulsar OS</span>
                            <p>All of that exists: a dock for your apps, an activities view for launching, quick settings in the corner, and Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V as normal. Your hands won't notice the difference; and when Wintux arrives it will go one step further.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Windows</span>
                            <p>C: drive, D: drive, letters for everything.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Pulsar OS</span>
                            <p>One folder tree: everything hangs from a single root, like branches of one tree. Your USB or second disk just appears in the file manager when plugged in — no letters to memorise.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Windows</span>
                            <p>Antivirus, updates that reboot when they want, "get even more out of Windows" nags.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Pulsar OS</span>
                            <p>None of that. You decide when to update. Antivirus isn't a daily worry: apps come from reviewed catalogues and the system is designed to contain them. No nags, no accounts, no ads in the start menu.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-compare-row">
                        <div class="ntl-compare-from">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Windows</span>
                            <p>Some professional apps are Windows-only.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
                        <div class="ntl-compare-to">
                            <span class="ntl-compare-label">On Pulsar OS</span>
                            <p>WinBoat runs real Windows apps as normal windows on your desktop, no reboot, no dual boot. And there is a free app for almost everything you do today.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <details class="ntl-deepdive">
                    <summary>Why is Linux free? What's the catch? (optional nerd bit)</summary>
                    <div class="ntl-deepdive-body">
                        <p>
                            Windows is a product someone sells you. Linux is a
                            commons: thousands of people and companies build it
                            together, because everyone benefits from a shared
                            foundation. Companies like Google and most of the
                            internet run on it.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The catch? There isn't one for you:
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <strong>No licences, no activations.</strong>{" "}
                                Install it on every computer you own.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <strong>No telemetry.</strong> Nothing phones
                                home with your data.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <strong>Old computers keep working.</strong>{" "}
                                No forced obsolescence: if the hardware turns
                                on, it can run Pulsar OS.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <strong>The one real cost:</strong> the couple
                                of hours of adjusting habits — and this page
                                exists to make those hours short.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Using Pulsar OS -->
        <section id="using-pulsar" class="ntl-section" aria-label="How to use Pulsar OS">
            <div class="container">
                <h2 class="section-title">Using Pulsar OS, day to day</h2>
                <p class="section-subtitle">
                    From the moment you switch it on, in the order you'll meet
                    it. This is the whole daily loop — there is nothing else to
                    memorise.
                </p>

                <div class="ntl-basics">
                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Day one: the hello, then the desktop</h3>
                            <p>
                                You boot, pick rEFInd or GRUB if asked, and the
                                Mac-style login screen welcomes you. The Welcome
                                app greets you with the classic "hello"
                                animation, sets the essentials and disappears. From
                                there: top bar with your app menus, dock below,
                                live wallpapers if you want them, and the Mac
                                boot chime — yes, it has one.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Installing apps: two ways</h3>
                            <p>
                                The App Store handles it all: Flathub, .deb
                                packages, AUR, GNOME extensions, even Sayri
                                skills — one search box, one install button. If
                                you prefer the Windows way, you can also download
                                an installer and open it by double-clicking. Both
                                roads work; nobody will force the other one on
                                you.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Updates: one screen, everything</h3>
                            <p>
                                A built-in updater watches the system and tells
                                you when there's something new — one screen,
                                everything updates, you choose when. The App
                                Store keeps its apps current too. Restarting to
                                update is rare, not weekly.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Files, clouds and drives</h3>
                            <p>
                                The Finder-style file manager with tags,
                                previews and search. Your clouds — Google Drive,
                                OneDrive, iCloud, Dropbox and any of 50+ others —
                                live in the sidebar, mounted like folders. A USB
                                stick or a second disk appears on its own when
                                you plug it in.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">5</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Backups: Time Machine, set and forget</h3>
                            <p>
                                Continuous snapshots of your files, saved to a
                                USB drive, a NAS or your cloud. If you delete
                                something or the disk dies, restore from
                                Recovery — or browse old versions of any folder
                                from the file manager.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">6</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Sayri: the orb at the top of the screen</h3>
                            <p>
                                An assistant pinned to the top of your screen,
                                like Siri but not trapped in a box. Talk or type,
                                in your language: it hears you (locally), speaks
                                back, connects to any AI provider or to a model
                                running on your own machine, and can act on the
                                system. It also answers from your phone over
                                Telegram or Discord.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">7</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>Keyboard: your call</h3>
                            <p>
                                Mac-style Cmd, or classic Ctrl+C? One setting
                                swaps every shortcut system-wide. Either way,
                                your muscle memory decides, not the OS.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">8</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>When something goes wrong</h3>
                            <p>
                                Recovery, from the boot menu: repair the system,
                                reinstall without losing your files, manage
                                disks, restore a Time Machine backup or even
                                browse the web from there — over the internet,
                                even if the main system won't start. For smaller
                                troubles, Sayri is the fastest way to fix
                                things.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="ntl-basics-item">
                        <span class="ntl-basics-num" aria-hidden="true">9</span>
                        <div>
                            <h3>The graphics drivers dance</h3>
                            <p>
                                One app handles it: it detects your GPU and
                                offers the right driver. NVIDIA, AMD or Intel,
                                no terminal, no wiki archaeology. It even keeps
                                control if a conflict needs a human decision.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <details class="ntl-deepdive">
                    <summary>What is "the terminal" and do I need it? (spoiler: no)</summary>
                    <div class="ntl-deepdive-body">
                        <p>
                            The terminal is a window where you type commands
                            instead of clicking. It is the old-school way to
                            control Linux, and it is why the internet is full of
                            scary-looking instructions.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            In Pulsar OS you can ignore it forever: the App
                            Store, Settings and Sayri cover daily life. But it
                            is there when you want it, and unlike the old days,
                            breaking something with it is hard — and Recovery
                            can undo it anyway.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- CTA -->
        <section class="ntl-cta" aria-label="Try Pulsar OS">
            <div class="container">
                <div class="ntl-cta-card">
                    <h2 class="ntl-cta-title">The best way to get it? Try it.</h2>
                    <p class="ntl-cta-desc">
                        Put it on a USB stick, boot it, poke around. Nothing on
                        your computer changes until you decide to install. Free,
                        obviously.
                    </p>
                    <div class="ntl-cta-actions">
                        <a href="https://downloads-os.inled.es" class="btn btn-primary">
                            Download the ISO
                            <svg class="btn-arrow" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                                <line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
                                <polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
                            </svg>
                        </a>
                        <a href="/help" class="btn btn-secondary">Read the install guide</a>
                        <a href="https://link.inled.es/discord" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-secondary">
                            Ask us anything
                        </a>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</Layout>

<style>
    /* Page-scoped styles: no collisions with global classes */
    .ntl-page {
        padding-bottom: 40px;
    }

    .ntl-hero {
        padding: 40px 0 30px 0;
        text-align: center;
    }

    .ntl-hero-inner {
        max-width: 720px;
        margin: 0 auto;
    }

    .ntl-title {
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-size: 3rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: -1.5px;
        color: var(--text-primary);
        margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    .ntl-lead {
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        line-height: 1.65;
        margin-bottom: 30px;
    }

    .ntl-hero-actions {
        display: flex;
        gap: 14px;
        justify-content: center;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .ntl-section {
        padding: 70px 0 10px 0;
    }

    .ntl-compare {
        max-width: 900px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 18px;
    }

    .ntl-compare-row {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 44px 1fr;
        align-items: stretch;
        gap: 0;
        background-color: var(--bg-card);
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 16px;
        overflow: hidden;
    }

    .ntl-compare-from,
    .ntl-compare-to {
        padding: 20px 24px;
    }

    .ntl-compare-to {
        background-color: var(--bg-card-hover);
    }

    .ntl-compare-label {
        display: block;
        font-size: 0.78rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.6px;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    .ntl-compare-from p,
    .ntl-compare-to p {
        font-size: 0.95rem;
        line-height: 1.55;
        color: var(--text-primary);
    }

    .ntl-compare-arrow {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        font-size: 1.1rem;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive {
        max-width: 900px;
        margin: 24px auto 0 auto;
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 16px;
        background-color: var(--bg-card);
        overflow: hidden;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive summary {
        list-style: none;
        cursor: pointer;
        padding: 18px 24px;
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-weight: 600;
        font-size: 0.98rem;
        color: var(--text-primary);
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 12px;
        user-select: none;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive summary::-webkit-details-marker {
        display: none;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive summary::after {
        content: "+";
        font-size: 1.3rem;
        font-weight: 400;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        transition: transform var(--transition-speed) ease;
        flex-shrink: 0;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive[open] summary::after {
        content: "−";
    }

    .ntl-deepdive summary:hover {
        color: var(--accent-blue);
    }

    .ntl-deepdive-body {
        padding: 4px 24px 22px 24px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    }

    .ntl-deepdive-body p {
        font-size: 0.94rem;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        line-height: 1.65;
        margin-top: 14px;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive-body ul {
        margin: 12px 0 0 0;
        padding-left: 20px;
    }

    .ntl-deepdive-body li {
        font-size: 0.94rem;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        line-height: 1.65;
        margin-bottom: 8px;
    }

    .ntl-basics {
        max-width: 900px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 16px;
    }

    .ntl-basics-item {
        display: flex;
        gap: 20px;
        align-items: flex-start;
        background-color: var(--bg-card);
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 16px;
        padding: 22px 26px;
    }

    .ntl-basics-num {
        flex-shrink: 0;
        width: 34px;
        height: 34px;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        border-radius: 50%;
        border: 1px solid var(--border-focus);
        color: var(--accent-blue);
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-weight: 700;
        font-size: 0.95rem;
    }

    .ntl-basics-item h3 {
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-size: 1.05rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: -0.3px;
        color: var(--text-primary);
        margin-bottom: 6px;
    }

    .ntl-basics-item p {
        font-size: 0.94rem;
        color: var(--text-secondary);
        line-height: 1.6;
    }

    .ntl-cta {
        padding: 70px 0 30px 0;
    }

    .ntl-cta-card {
        max-width: 900px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        text-align: center;
        background-color: var(--bg-card);
        border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
        border-radius: 24px;
        padding: 52px 36px;
    }

    .ntl-cta-title {
        font-family: var(--font-display);
        font-size: 1.8rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: -0.6px;
        color: var(--text-primary);
        margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .ntl-cta-desc {
